Umm, I, at least, didn't "see flagrant design flaws smack in the
middle of the game"; I saw a profitable but boring and time-consuming
game option (which I have yet to exercise) that, to me at least,
doesn't seem all *that* abusive.

If your major argument is that farming is bad because it dissuades
newcomers from sticking with Nethack, do you (or anyone out there)
have any numbers to support this view? Has anyone taken a poll of new
players and asked them what they think?

Would it be possible (I have no idea how difficult this would be) to
pull some statistics off of NAO (although a highly self-selected and
possibly not totally representative sample...)? How many new players
actually set up a farm (using, say, pudding kill counts as a
substitute metric) within their first X games (or before their first
ascension, or whatever)? What percentage of those who tried farming
are still playing after __ (fill in timeframe of choice), and is it
different from the percentage among those that have never farmed?

In all probability a significant number of people who try Nethack do
not become dedicated players, regardless of whether they have ever so
much as heard of pudding farming. It would also not surprise me if a
large fraction of players try farming at least once. In this case,
once would see a number of people who play for a while, farm a bit,
and leave, which could give the impression that they are leaving
*because* of farming, even if those who have farmed are equally likely
to keep playing.

> [snip] When the capacity
> exists to obtain infinite items, the game itself reduced from
> multi-dimensional to one-dimensional, and this is _never_ good.

And that's why playing in wizard mode isn't any fun. But even pudding
farming is not one-dimensional, or all farmers would always ascend and
no-one would be annoyed by stepping into puddingopolis bones files.

> [snip] despite all the other
> types of body armor available in the game, the "choice" that should
> normally result in significant variety really ends up being only a coin
> flip... gray or silver?

My personal opinion is that a game of the complexity of Nethack, in
which every single decision requires mulling through dozens of
possible pros and cons, would be far too tedious. There are so many
other things in Nethack that require careful judgment and compromise
that having a few clear "best-choice" cases is, on the whole, a
benefit.

